{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAQaDVovYste42Vekz5v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`Hijack()` was rejecting the connection as soon as headers had been sent, even with no body written yet. WebSocket libraries often send the 101 \"Switching Protocols\" response first and then take over the connection, so they hit `errHijackAlreadyWritten`. I fixed this in `response_writer.go`.\n\n- **Fix:** `Hijack()` now rejects only after body bytes have been written. Headers alone no longer block it.\n- **Test:** I added a case to `response_writer_test.go` that sends a 101 header and then hijacks; it must succeed. The existing case that hijacks after a body write still gets `errHijackAlreadyWritten`.\n- **Results:** `go test ./...` passes and `gofmt` reports nothing.\n\nI didn't test this against a real WebSocket library. The changes are uncommitted and HEAD is detached; tell me if you want me to commit with your message."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":165,"cache_read_input_tokens":35938,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":165,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e7f7120-0e7e-436d-8a68-a53b34f57f42","uuid":"0c7ef091-b741-4165-90ba-417bf0959e8c","timestamp":"2026-10-18T21:24:25.820Z","request_id":"req_011CgAQaDHetrw6P3ryLrJmD"}
